00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:21.647
He goes, "You post your own Instagram?"

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:26.610
So the answer is yes
and I write all the copy

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.880
and I think people realize
how bad my grammar is

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:30.590
so I think they understand that

00:19:30.590 --> 00:19:31.820
but I still don't think they believe it

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:33.200
or they think somebody does it.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:36.330
I think the biggest thing that
I think about when scaling

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:39.534
is what do you keep
and what do you let go?

00:19:39.534 --> 00:19:41.640
And that has to be individual.

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:44.151
The way you scale is by realizing

00:19:44.151 --> 00:19:47.700
five people doing an 80 of your 100

00:19:47.700 --> 00:19:50.190
is better than just you doing 100.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:53.032
And so first and foremost,
it starts with ambition.

00:19:53.032 --> 00:19:56.350
So for example, a lot
of people say shit like

00:19:56.350 --> 00:19:58.950
oh don't look at Gary Vee,
he has 20 fucking people,

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:00.592
that's why,

00:20:00.592 --> 00:20:03.890
but what they don't
realize is first of all,

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:05.450
and Andy, I'm trying to look for you,

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:09.190
but the reality is in its truth
and a lot of the teams here,

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:12.240
in its truth, we could
be remarkably efficient

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:13.840
in doing what we're
doing with six or seven.

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:17.210
The reason I have 20 is
I'm training a lot of them

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:20.760
to infiltrate the overall
organization in the long term.

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:22.330
I have 20 because I want,

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:24.080
'cause Baben right now is working,

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.720
has been working, if you haven't noticed

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:26.710
he hasn't been around in the content,

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:29.300
he's working with VaynerTalent,
one of our divisions

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:33.430
and ONE37PM is something I
have enormous ambition for,

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:35.403
whether it's Saneen or Syd, Jake,

00:20:36.458 --> 00:20:37.291
there's so many people on the team here

00:20:37.291 --> 00:20:38.730
that I know can go and dominate there

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:40.355
but I'd like them to get incubated

00:20:40.355 --> 00:20:42.150
for six months to a year or two

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:43.940
and really fucking know it.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:47.150
So my ambition is to buy the New York Jets

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:50.677
and so creating scale and
being inefficient with 20

00:20:50.677 --> 00:20:52.540
and we are plenty fucking inefficient

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:53.720
with the amount of people we have,

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:55.610
and that's why everybody's smiling,

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.140
is still ROI positive

00:20:57.140 --> 00:20:59.757
because I'm gonna scale them to the 900.

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:03.450
I think you need to know
what your ambition is,

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:04.283
first and foremost

00:21:04.283 --> 00:21:05.740
and then I think you need to hold on

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:07.320
to what you value the most.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:09.930
I on Instagram and Twitter,

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:11.580
every single one of those posts are me

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:12.850
and I need it to be me

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:14.153
and I have to have it be me

00:21:14.153 --> 00:21:16.520
and it means a lot to me that it's me

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:17.690
and that's what means something to me.

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:20.190
And so that's just the way it is.

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:21.620
So I think you need to think

00:21:21.620 --> 00:21:24.640
about what you want to
be doing with your time

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:25.970
and what you value

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:27.809
and then you start
building a team around you

00:21:27.809 --> 00:21:30.890
that has complementary
skills to the things,

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:32.180
and I go one of two ways,

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:33.540
either they're very like you,

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:35.100
and for me, to what you mentioned,

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:36.490
I saw people shaking their heads,

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:39.040
to me, where my team
has to be very like me

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:40.927
is they have to be nice.

00:21:40.927 --> 00:21:42.740
Not super complicated,

00:21:42.740 --> 00:21:45.870
I'm completely suffocated in negativity.

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:48.210
Thus, if I'm the fucking engine,

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:50.440
I don't care if somebody's
the greatest growth hacker

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:53.130
or can design, is fucking Pablo Picasso.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:55.330
If Pablo Picasso's a
fucking dick, he's out.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:59.500
That's what has to be like me.

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:01.910
Everything else has to be not like me,

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:04.130
like you have to be able to read an email

00:22:06.027 --> 00:22:07.580
and many other things.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.710
And so I think that ego

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:15.464
is what holds back people from growing.

00:22:15.464 --> 00:22:16.850
They like the feeling,

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:20.553
in companies, and personal
brands are tough 'cause it's you.

00:22:20.553 --> 00:22:24.390
So I'm not comfortable with
somebody writing on my behalf.

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:26.817
I have to say everything I say.

00:22:26.817 --> 00:22:28.360
Every piece of content

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:30.723
is transcription from my words.

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:34.630
People are like, I
always read the reviews,

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:36.199
they're like, oh my God,

00:22:36.199 --> 00:22:38.411
this book sounds just like
you, Gary Vee. I'm like, right.

00:22:38.411 --> 00:22:43.140
It's literally, the ghost writer
is just making it grammar.

00:22:43.140 --> 00:22:44.322
It's transcriptions.

00:22:44.322 --> 00:22:47.127
So I think that's what
you have to think about.

00:22:47.127 --> 00:22:49.710
- [Brock] Okay, so I
don't have to feel guilty

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:53.060
about my Slack channels just
being really unorganized?

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:55.723
So you really focus on
speed and people like you.

00:22:55.723 --> 00:23:00.480
- Yeah, to me, you need to be you.

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:02.710
Everybody's gonna always win most

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:04.870
if they lean in more to what they are

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:06.510
instead of trying to do something

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:08.470
that they see somebody else doing well

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:10.310
and be ideological about it.

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.390
In art and sports,

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:16.290
we've accepted that we're
not Lebron or Beyonce.

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:19.450
For some reason in entrepreneurship,

00:23:19.450 --> 00:23:23.830
we have this false feeling that we can be.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:27.480
We have not layered the talent card

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:30.330
into the entrepreneurial creative land

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:32.870
to the level that I think we need to.

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:33.880
Everybody just thinks

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:36.260
that they're gonna make a
million dollar business,

00:23:36.260 --> 00:23:38.089
that it's so easy

00:23:38.089 --> 00:23:41.560
in a way that we don't with
putting out a rap album

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:42.933
or playing basketball

00:23:42.933 --> 00:23:45.400
and that's a day of reckoning

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:48.740
that needs to happen in this
space to help everybody.

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:52.240
For me, I'm only comfortable in chaos.

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:55.170
There's a report card
hanging there in my office

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:57.340
for a couple reasons.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:23:58.470
One, I just think it's hilarious

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:03.460
but more importantly, I'm
not going to be organized,

00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:04.980
that's not what I get paid to do,

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:06.390
that's not who I am.

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.050
I feel no bad about that whatsoever.

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:09.970
I want to find people

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:11.290
that are comfortable in our organization,

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:14.260
and I'll tell you, Andy,
who's my right hand, the guy,

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:17.100
I've watched him, not get less organized,

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:19.350
I've watched him and
everybody else on my team

00:24:19.350 --> 00:24:20.963
prioritize better

00:24:20.963 --> 00:24:24.800
because they start off more organized

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:28.130
and then I systematically in osmosis

00:24:28.130 --> 00:24:31.623
take out of them to
not focus on dumb shit,

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.141
even though they're the one in charge

00:24:35.141 --> 00:24:38.810
and they have to care more than I do.

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:40.080
But no, I don't think you

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:45.950
beating yourself up around your DNA

00:24:46.180 --> 00:24:49.488
is such a negative exercise.

00:24:49.488 --> 00:24:53.580
What you need to do is figure
out what you naturally do best

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:55.010
and quadruple down on that

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:56.680
and then build infrastructure around it.

00:24:56.680 --> 00:25:00.080
I genuinely believe that, the end.

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:02.337
That's why I love Q &amp; A.

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:06.720
That's why I struggle with
some of the stuff I put out

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:09.020
because I hate that I
can't have full context.

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:14.040
It's why I love Q &amp; A. I need the context.

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:16.740
The best advice I give are
the people I know the best.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:19.683
Questions?

00:25:20.950 --> 00:25:22.400
Yep, here I'll give you mine.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:26.338
- So your personal brand is
- Who are you,

00:25:26.338 --> 00:25:27.374
and where are you from?

00:25:27.374 --> 00:25:29.350
- [Alan] Oh, my name's Alan
Baruby, I'm from Saco, Maine

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:32.420
and I was wondering about
you branding your name.

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:37.220
So did you just say, hey
I'm gonna brand my name?

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:39.210
Was there another business
idea that you thought

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:40.980
maybe it was gonna be the brand instead?

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:44.119
'Cause it really puts a
lot of emphasis on you

00:25:44.119 --> 00:25:46.420
and when you brand your name,

00:25:46.420 --> 00:25:48.020
you're kind of stuck with that, right?

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:49.203
You can't change it?

00:25:52.307 --> 00:25:55.394
- Here give me this real quick.
You can't change it as in,

00:25:55.394 --> 00:25:57.920
to me I love the name because
I actually think it gives you

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:00.139
disproportionate
flexibility to do anything.

00:26:00.139 --> 00:26:03.930
As a human, if you go from
wine guy to social media guy

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.710
to whatever you want to do next,

00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:07.650
I think it gives you that flexibility

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:10.027
whereas if you're like the photo expert

00:26:10.027 --> 00:26:11.710
and then you want to write,

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:13.210
you're like, oh fuck.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:16.210
So when I look to people go
literal with their brands

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:18.895
versus I'm Sally Thompson or Rick Jones,

00:26:18.895 --> 00:26:22.290
I think sure you need to do a little work

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:24.280
but what I mean by little work

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:26.763
is your actions become your brand.

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:29.930
If you go read the early,

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:31.420
if you go back to 2009

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:32.360
and go on Twitter,

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:34.720
people shit on me for
but you're the wine guy,

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:36.579
you can't start a social media agency.

00:26:36.579 --> 00:26:39.030
I'm like why?

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:42.250
I'm like over time my actions
will make me that brand.

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:44.880
Today, way more people
know me as an entrepreneur

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:46.660
than they do as a wine guy.

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:49.290
But the first year of my
existence and the transition,

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:52.830
I got shit on at scale
for making that transition

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:56.010
but that's like Marky Mark
becoming Mark Wahlberg.

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:58.720
The Rock is less of a wrestler today

00:26:58.720 --> 00:26:59.973
than he was 10 years ago.

00:27:00.814 --> 00:27:02.490
So what I always tell people

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:04.330
is whatever you are now,

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:06.313
your execution's gonna define you.

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:11.260
So to me, I'm not scare
of it for that reason.

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:13.320
- [Alan] And you didn't get
stuck to be the wine guy,

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.600
you just wrapped everything
around wine at that point,

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:18.470
I guess Gary Vee could be
anything at that point?

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:23.161
- Yeah, and I think again,
there's so much that goes in,

00:27:23.161 --> 00:27:24.158
there's so many funny,

00:27:24.158 --> 00:27:26.050
first of all, the Gary Vee
thing is really funny to me.

00:27:26.050 --> 00:27:28.140
First of all, it's a terrible
name in the way I spelled it

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:30.180
because there's two silent E's.

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:32.342
It's just a bad idea for a brand.

00:27:32.342 --> 00:27:34.610
Number two, it's funny how it went down.

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:35.910
I tried to register for Twitter

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.718
four months earlier than the
first time I registered for it.

00:27:38.718 --> 00:27:42.190
Literally I saw it and I
tried to register for it

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:44.160
and the wifi in London didn't work

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:45.660
and I just was like fuck it

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:48.020
and I got back to it four months later

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:49.745
and the first time I had just Gary

00:27:49.745 --> 00:27:51.690
and the second time it was taken

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:54.410
and I never, talk about

00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:55.810
I love how life works,

00:27:55.810 --> 00:28:00.810
in my life, I had never, ever
tried to register Gary v-e-e,

00:28:01.700 --> 00:28:02.533
I don't even known where it came from,

00:28:02.533 --> 00:28:03.637
I have no idea why I did it

00:28:03.637 --> 00:28:05.670
and it ended up being fucking Twitter.

00:28:05.670 --> 00:28:08.870
Plurk and Shmirk and
all the other MySpace,

00:28:08.870 --> 00:28:11.560
all the other shit, Friendster,

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:13.850
but this was the one that put me on

00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:15.653
and then that became me.

00:28:15.653 --> 00:28:18.930
So I didn't think about the brand name.

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:19.763
Names to me,

00:28:19.763 --> 00:28:21.570
when I watch entrepreneurs and individuals

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:23.630
think about names too much I laugh

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:26.368
because I'm like, what does Facebook mean,

00:28:26.368 --> 00:28:27.982
what does Nike mean?

00:28:27.982 --> 00:28:31.610
Nothing means shit when it first starts.

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:33.393
The execution defines it.

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:37.593
McDonald's is a Irish pub, right?

00:28:38.849 --> 00:28:42.550
So it's the execution.

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:45.160
And so that's how I think
about branding your name.

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:46.310
If today you're a lawyer

00:28:46.310 --> 00:28:49.890
and in four years you want to be a clown

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:51.156
and then nine years later

00:28:51.156 --> 00:28:53.690
you want to be the
foremost expert in bourbon

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:55.930
it is your execution and your talent

00:28:55.930 --> 00:28:57.330
that will define that over time

00:28:57.330 --> 00:29:01.057
I the same way that Justin
Timberlake isn't from N'Sync.

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:06.210
Your skills are what predicate the outcome

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:08.820
and I think people get caught up in a name

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:12.090
which is a quick tell of not
seeing what actually plays out

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:13.293
when building brand.

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:16.260
- I didn't even choose my name,

00:29:16.260 --> 00:29:17.100
it just kind of happened

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:18.967
and then I've had to roll with it

00:29:18.967 --> 00:29:20.700
but I could choose my
mantra, what I'm all about.

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:22.450
I have the best day ever

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:23.890
and I give everyone high fives

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:26.730
and I'm always positive and
that's what I could decide

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:27.980
and that's really all that matters.

00:29:27.980 --> 00:29:30.660
The name just kind of comes
with the Shonduras, Dr. Duras,

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:34.363
I might get into medical, I
don't know, whatever sticks.

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:36.780
- Questions?

00:29:36.780 --> 00:29:38.240
Who's got the mics?

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:39.073
Go ahead, go ahead.

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:43.000
- [Emory] Hey, my name is Emory

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:44.540
from Charlotte, North Carolina.

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:47.090
My question is what was your biggest

00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:49.140
internal or external struggle

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:51.070
with building your brand
from the beginning,

00:29:51.070 --> 00:29:52.643
when you were just starting out?

00:29:52.643 --> 00:29:54.820
- Hey, we talked for five
minutes before I got food,

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:56.191
what's up?

00:29:56.191 --> 00:29:57.070
- [Emory] We did, what's up?

00:29:58.479 --> 00:30:00.854
- For me it was that scalability.

00:30:00.854 --> 00:30:03.040
Most of my first business ventures

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:04.630
were very heavy on me

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:06.120
and it wasn't until I met Holladay

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:07.280
that I didn't realize

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:10.060
it's not just a matter of working
harder and working harder,

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:11.840
you need to build a team.

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:14.120
And we just talked about
how to do that correctly

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:16.820
and that was my number one
thing that was hard is because

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:18.509
and you noticed earlier I stressed

00:30:18.509 --> 00:30:20.550
giving away creative control.

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:22.710
It's because for me, that
was the hardest thing

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:23.870
because I am a creative

00:30:23.870 --> 00:30:25.635
and I want my videos to look like this

00:30:25.635 --> 00:30:27.060
and my emails to look like
this and everything else.

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:27.893
And I'm like Gary,

00:30:27.893 --> 00:30:30.810
I have the things that, my
social media's me, I post that,

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:34.240
but everything else I need
to have a team backing me

00:30:34.240 --> 00:30:35.560
and I need to trust in my team,

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:38.530
I need to train them to do what I think

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:39.490
is right for the company.

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:40.890
But when it comes down to it,

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:42.730
they need to do what their gut check is

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:44.150
and I need to trust them on that

